# Stage 49.9.2 - review ownership.
#
# CODEOWNERS only does anything once branch protection on `main` requires a
# review from the matching code owner before merge - that's a repository
# setting (Settings > Branches > Branch protection rules > "Require review
# from Code Owners") an org owner has to turn on; this file cannot turn it on
# by itself. See docs/security/secure-development-lifecycle.md, section 49.9.2,
# for the exact settings to apply, marked [needs decision: org owner].
#
# git log shows exactly one contributor identity on this repository today
# (github.com/Vishalnayak226, the repo owner). That is why every row below
# names @Vishalnayak226 rather than a team: inventing a fictitious team
# handle here would make this file look enforced when it is not, and a
# CODEOWNERS entry that names a team GitHub cannot resolve silently stops
# blocking merges instead of failing loudly. When a second reviewer joins,
# split the areas below across real accounts or a real GitHub team (Settings
# > Teams) and update this file in the same change that grants them repo
# access - do not let review ownership and repo access drift apart.
#
# Order matters: GitHub uses the LAST matching pattern, so the most specific
# paths are listed after the general fallback.

